# Line-ending normalization.
#
# Why this file exists: on 2026-08-16 this working tree reported 652 modified files
# (91,900 insertions) containing ZERO authored changes. 624 of them were a pure
# LF->CRLF flip in the working tree while every HEAD blob was LF; the remaining 28
# were that same flip plus rustfmt reordering. Without normalization rules, git
# treats a CRLF worktree copy of an LF blob as a whole-file modification, so the
# repo looks like it holds hundreds of files of work in progress when it holds none.
#
# `text=auto` lets git decide what is text, and normalizes text to LF *in the
# repository* on commit. Checkout still uses the platform's native convention
# (governed by core.autocrlf), so Windows contributors are unaffected — this only
# guarantees CRLF is never recorded, which is what makes the phantom diff impossible.
# Deliberately no `eol=` override: this repo carries a large C#/Visual Studio subtree
# (.cs/.sln/.csproj) where forcing LF would be a gratuitous behavior change.
* text=auto

# Binary assets — never touch these. git's heuristic usually gets them right, but
# the cost of a wrong guess is a corrupted fixture, so they are declared explicitly.
*.bin   binary
*.pdu   binary
*.data  binary
*.bmp   binary
*.png   binary
*.ico   binary

# PEM is TEXT, not binary. Marking it `binary` is an easy mistake with a real cost:
# git then stores the worktree bytes verbatim, so a CRLF worktree copy would have
# ADDED 22 (cert) and 28 (key) CR bytes to fixtures that are LF in HEAD — measured
# here on 2026-08-16 before it was committed. Normalizing them to LF is correct and
# keeps the test certs byte-stable across platforms.
*.pem   text

# Shader sources are text.
*.frag  text
*.vert  text
